'''alphabet''' (al′fa-bet), ''n.'' [First in early mod. E. (earlier expressed by ''a-b-c'', q. v.); = D. ''alfabet'' = G. ''alphabet'' = Sw. Dan. ''alfabet'' = F. ''alphabet'' = Sp. Pg. ''alfabeto'', Pg. also ''alphabeto'', = It. ''alfabeto'' = Russ. ''alfabetŭ'' = Pol. ''alfabet'', etc., < LL. ''alphabetum'' (earlier ''alpha et beta''), < Gr. ἀλφάβητος, < ἄλφα + βῆτα, the names of the first two letters of the Greek alphabet, corresponding to ''a'' and ''b'': see ''alpha'' and ''beta''. Cf. ''a-b-c'', ''abecedarian'', and ''futhork''.] '''1.''' The letters of a language arranged in the customary order; the series of letters or characters which form the elements of written language. See the articles on the different letters, ''A'', ''B'', ''C'', etc.
{{smaller block|From the character of the ''alphabet'' employed, the science of Greek epigraphy professes to be able to determine approximately the date and the place of origin of inscriptions.
{{right|''Isaac Taylor'', The Alphabet, II. 3.}}}}
